<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple Computer//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  TWO COPIES OF THIS FILE SHIP: this one, and a byte-identical one at
  /System/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.openssh.sshd.plist under the same Label.
  That is not belt-and-braces. It is the whole reason sshd starts at all, and
  it took two wrong answers to get to.

  THE STOCK IMAGE HAS NO /Library/LaunchDaemons AND launchd NEVER LOOKS
  THERE. Read off the real decrypted 12H1006 root filesystem
  (048-37456-138.dmg, RootFSKey from theapplewiki):

    * /Library/LaunchDaemons DOES NOT EXIST. /Library ships Application
      Support, Audio, Caches, Filesystems, Internet Plug-Ins, Keychains,
      LaunchAgents, Logs, Managed Preferences, MobileDevice, Preferences,
      Printers, Updates -- and no LaunchDaemons.
    * /System/Library/LaunchDaemons has 174 plists.
    * The device's /sbin/launchd contains the string
      /System/Library/LaunchDaemons and contains /Library/LaunchDaemons
      NOWHERE.

  So how does every jailbreak package -- cydia, openssh, freemem-watcher,
  tssagent, fuzzyparrot -- ship into /Library/LaunchDaemons and work? THE
  UNTETHER LOADS IT BY HAND. misc/untether.bin, the payload this project
  ships, carries this in plain text:

      echo 'really jailbroken';
      ls /Library/LaunchDaemons | while read a; do launchctl load /Library/LaunchDaemons/$a; done;
      ls /etc/rc.d | while read a; do /etc/rc.d/$a; done;

  (tihmstar's /etc/rc.d/daemonload, which stageEtasonatv() has always staged,
  is the same launchctl load wrapped in orphan_commander -- so it happens
  twice. Stock tvOS has no /etc/rc.d directory either; that is a jailbreak
  construct run only by the loop above.)

  Which means everything in /Library/LaunchDaemons runs ONLY on a boot where
  the untether ran -- never on a TETHER boot, where the patched kernel already
  makes AMFI permissive so the untether is neither needed nor triggered. That
  is the configuration sshd was being tested in every single time.

  THE EARLIER SELF-CORRECTION WAS ALSO WRONG, and that is worth recording.
  The first evidence for this was the restore ramdisk's /sbin/launchd, which
  was dismissed as "the wrong binary -- not the booted device's". It is the
  SAME BINARY: both are 239,536 bytes and both hash to
  dfd35edf9a66a8408506e1da446d52b07cc1d11ac7fe83cb6f9fb7bbffedfcbb. The
  packages shipping to /Library looked like a refutation and were not; they
  are evidence about iOS generally, where launchd does scan that directory.

  THIS DELIBERATELY OVERWRITES THE openssh PACKAGE'S OWN PLIST, at the same
  path and under the same Label, so there is exactly one sshd job rather than
  two fighting over port 22. Our package is staged after the debcache merge
  (stageBlackb0xTree() calls stageDebcache() then stageBlackb0xPackage()), so
  the later write wins.

  WHY. On the first device boot where the system partition merge had fully
  landed, sshd did not come up. The package's plist is socket-activated:

      inetdCompatibility  Wait = false
      Sockets  Listeners  SockServiceName = "ssh"
      StandardErrorPath   /dev/null

  Three things wrong with that here, and the third is why the first two went
  unnoticed for so long.

    1. `inetdCompatibility` is a launchd 1.0 facility. This device runs the
       XPC-era launchd, where it is at best vestigial. A job that is never
       triggered never fails, so there is nothing in any log to find.
    2. `SockServiceName = "ssh"` is resolved through /etc/services. We ship no
       /etc/services and do not control what the stock tvOS image carries, so
       the port that job binds is not a thing this project can assert. A name
       lookup that fails takes the whole job with it.
    3. StandardErrorPath is /dev/null. Whatever sshd had to say about any of
       this was discarded before it was written. On a device whose only output
       channel took a week of work to obtain, throwing stderr away is the
       expensive part of the bug, not a detail.

  So: no socket activation, no name lookup, and both streams go to a real file
  on the system partition. sshd is started directly and kept alive, which is
  what the original blackb0x did too -- its disassembled launchd staged its own
  com.openssh.sshd.plist as a loose top-level file rather than relying on the
  package's (see misc/README.md's record of what the original installed).

  Program vs ProgramArguments[0] is not a mistake. launchd execs `Program` but
  passes `ProgramArguments` as argv, so the wrapper runs with $0 = /usr/sbin/sshd
  and $@ = -D. /usr/libexec/sshd-keygen-wrapper is a /bin/sh script that
  generates the three host keys into /etc/ssh if they are missing and then
  `exec /usr/sbin/sshd $@`. That matters twice over: no host keys ship (only
  moduli, ssh_config and sshd_config do), and /etc is /private/etc on the
  SYSTEM partition, so the keys it writes land on the volume that accepts
  writes rather than the one that refuses them. Expect the first start to take
  a moment while it generates them.

  `-D` rather than `-i`: -i is inetd mode, which expects an already-accepted
  connection on stdin and is meaningless without the socket activation above.
  -D is "daemon, but do not detach", which is what a launchd job wants -- a
  process that forks away would be reaped and restarted forever.

  UsePrivilegeSeparation is `no` in the shipped sshd_config, so this needs
  neither /var/empty nor anything else on the data partition. That is load
  bearing right now: /var is not populated until the first-boot postinstall
  daemon migrates the stage, and a job that needed /var/empty would fail on
  exactly the boot where someone is trying to SSH in to find out why.

  TWO DECLARATIONS HOLD THIS OVERRIDE IN PLACE, and they guard different
  directions. DEBIAN/control carries `Replaces: openssh`, which is what lets
  dpkg legitimately write this path even though openssh's .list claims it --
  without it, any on-device `apt-get install`/upgrade of OUR package aborts
  with "trying to overwrite ... which is also in package openssh". And
  openssh itself is held (`Status: hold ok installed`, set at bake time by
  computePreinstalledPackages()), so apt never resolves openssh and never
  restores its own version over this one. Replaces lets us win the write;
  the hold stops the write from happening in the other direction. Removing
  either one reintroduces a failure that only appears on a device, weeks
  later, the first time someone runs apt.

  Note that the bake hand-writes dpkg state rather than running dpkg, so the
  path stays listed in openssh.list; a real `dpkg --unpack` honouring
  Replaces would have moved it to ours. That simplification is harmless
  while openssh is held, and `Replaces` is what makes it harmless if it ever
  is not.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
      <string>com.openssh.sshd</string>
    <key>Program</key>
      <string>/usr/libexec/sshd-keygen-wrapper</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
      <array>
        <string>/usr/sbin/sshd</string>
        <string>-D</string>
      </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
      <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
      <true/>
    <key>SessionCreate</key>
      <true/>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
      <string>/usr/share/blackb0x/sshd.out.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
      <string>/usr/share/blackb0x/sshd.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
